e\n1897 |     values : np.ndarray\n1898 |         An array of object dtype that will be inferred to have\n1899 |         `inferred_dtype`\n1900 | \n1901 |     Examples\n1902 |     --------\n1903 |     >>> from pandas._libs import lib\n1904 |     >>>\n1905 |     >>> def test_something(any_skipna_inferred_dtype):\n1906 |     ...     inferred_dtype, values = any_skipna_inferred_dtype\n1907 |     ...     # will pass\n1908 |     ...     assert lib.infer_dtype(values, skipna=True) == inferred_dtype\n1909 |     \"\"\"\n1910 |     inferred_dtype, values = request.param\n1911 |     values = np.array(values, dtype=object)  # object dtype to avoid casting\n1912 | \n1913 |     # correctness of inference tested in tests/dtypes/test_inference.py\n1914 |     return inferred_dtype, values\n```\n\nFound function `pandas/core/strings/accessor.py:StringMethods._validate`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n221 | def _validate(data):\n222 |         \"\"\"\n223 |         Auxiliary function for StringMethods, infers and checks dtype of data.\n224 | \n225 |         This is a \"first line of defence\" at the creation of the StringMethods-\n226 |         object, and just checks that the dtype is in the\n227 |         *union* of the allowed types over all string methods below; this\n228 |         restriction is then refined on a per-method basis using the decorator\n229 |         @forbid_nonstring_types (more info in the corresponding docstring).\n230 | \n231 |         This really should exclude all series/index with any non-string values,\n232 |         but that isn't practical for performance reasons until we have a str\n233 |         dtype (GH 9343 / 13877)\n234 | \n235 |         Parameters\n236 |         ----------\n237 |         data : The content of the Series\n238 | \n239 |         Returns\n240 |         -------\n241 |         dtype : inferred dtype of data\n242 |         \"\"\"\n243 |         if isinstance(data, ABCMultiIndex):\n244 |             raise